`debian/build.sh [OUT]` builds the package of the committed HEAD for the
machine's own architecture in a Docker container (on a PC an amd64 test
build; ARCH=arm64 there would emulate arm64 and take long), into
../libva-v4l2-request-deb. The arm64 package for the phone comes from
GitHub Actions (.github/workflows/deb.yml), which runs the same script on
an arm64 machine on every push to the lisbon branch and on request.

The packaging (debian/ and .github/workflows/deb.yml) and the README
section about the fork are commits of their own on the lisbon branch; no
driver commit touches these files, so driver commits can go upstream
(https://xff.cz/git/libva-v4l2_request) unchanged.

No Debian package ships panfrost_drv_video.so or panthor_drv_video.so
(Mesa's VA-API frontend has no Panfrost or Panthor driver), so the links
need neither Conflicts nor Replaces. Should Mesa add one, dpkg refuses to
overwrite the file, and the links have to go.
